{"type":"assistant","uuid":"ec0fe07e-df46-4d6d-a028-b2bf68d39cd5","session_id":"c63b4da7-5b0a-4953-b512-27157734736c","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"ec0fe07e-df46-4d6d-a028-b2bf68d39cd5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fix is applied. Here's a summary:\n\n---\n\n## Bug Analysis\n\n**URL:** `https://example.com?=`\n\n**Root cause:** In `UrlParts`, when the query string is just `=` (empty name, empty value), Java's `\"=\".split(\"=\")` returns an **empty array** `[]` — because both the before and after parts are empty, and trailing empty strings are discarded by default. The original code did `parts.get(0)` which throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`.\n\n## Fix Applied (2 methods in `RemoveQueriesModule.java`)\n\n### `getQueryName(int index)` — before:\n```java\nreturn queries.get(index).split(\"=\")[0];\n```\n### after:\n```java\nString[] parts = queries.get(index).split(\"=\");\nif (parts.length == 0) return \"\";\nreturn parts[0];\n```\n\n### `getQueryValue(int index)` — before:\n```java\nif (split.length == 1) return \"\";\n```\n### after:\n```java\nif (split.length < 2) return \"\";\n```\n(Changed from `== 1` to `< 2` to also handle the `length == 0` edge case.)\n\nThis ensures that for a URL like `https://example.com?=`, the empty query is handled gracefully — returning an empty name and empty value instead of crashing."}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":29751,"output_tokens":311,"cache_read_input_tokens":28512,"total_tokens":30062}}}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","uuid":"d4c03475-bbe4-4489-a3df-549c88c35676","session_id":"c63b4da7-5b0a-4953-b512-27157734736c","is_error":false,"duration_ms":81764,"duration_api_ms":67780,"num_turns":14,"result":"The fix is applied. Here's a summary:\n\n---\n\n## Bug Analysis\n\n**URL:** `https://example.com?=`\n\n**Root cause:** In `UrlParts`, when the query string is just `=` (empty name, empty value), Java's `\"=\".split(\"=\")` returns an **empty array** `[]` — because both the before and after parts are empty, and trailing empty strings are discarded by default. The original code did `parts.get(0)` which throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`.\n\n## Fix Applied (2 methods in `RemoveQueriesModule.java`)\n\n### `getQueryName(int index)` — before:\n```java\nreturn queries.get(index).split(\"=\")[0];\n```\n### after:\n```java\nString[] parts = queries.get(index).split(\"=\");\nif (parts.length == 0) return \"\";\nreturn parts[0];\n```\n\n### `getQueryValue(int index)` — before:\n```java\nif (split.length == 1) return \"\";\n```\n### after:\n```java\nif (split.length < 2) return \"\";\n```\n(Changed from `== 1` to `< 2` to also handle the `length == 0` edge case.)\n\nThis ensures that for a URL like `https://example.com?=`, the empty query is handled gracefully — returning an empty name and empty value instead of crashing.","usage":{"input_tokens":363597,"output_tokens":5508,"cache_read_input_tokens":324192,"total_tokens":369105},"permission_denials":[]}
